<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Leipzig</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of the Nations, Völkerschlacht · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-sixth-coalition">War of the Sixth Coalition</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, or Battle of the Nations, was fought on 16–19 October 1813 around Leipzig in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. The armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in a battle involving some 600,000 soldiers, and his German allies began to desert him. It was the largest battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, ended French power east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and led to the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig.jpg" alt="Battle of Leipzig" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Moshkov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MoshkovVI_SrazhLeypcigomGRM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812 ended in disaster, and in June 1813 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a> routed the French at Vitoria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The allies regrouped as the Sixth Coalition, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, Russia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, the United Kingdom and smaller German states whose rulers no longer felt loyal to the French emperor. Napoleon raised another large army, but it consisted largely of teenagers and inexperienced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, and his cavalry was weak. The Coalition followed the Trachenberg Plan, defeating his marshals with weaker forces while wearing out his own corps in chases across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>Two attempts to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> failed, at Großbeeren and Dennewitz. Napoleon withdrew westward and gathered his army around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> to protect his supply lines, while the Coalition armies converged on the city. He was outnumbered but held Leipzig and its bridges, which let him shift troops between sectors faster than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> could.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The fighting lasted from 16 to 19 October 1813. On the first day the French won small victories at Lindenau and Wachau and had a reverse at Möckern, and the day ended in a draw. Reinforcements then favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>: 145,000 arrived by 17 October, against 14,000 for the French. On 18 October Napoleon offered to surrender his fortresses on the Oder and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in return for an armistice and a withdrawal behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. Alexander I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> and Frederick William III declined.</p><p>On 19 October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> attacked the retreating French, while Marshal Oudinot's rearguard of 30,000 fought street by street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. A corporal blew up the only bridge over the Elster while it was crowded with retreating troops, and the rout cost thousands of French lives and 30,000 prisoners. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-poniatowski">Prince Józef Poniatowski</a> drowned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> was the bloodiest battle of the Napoleonic Wars, with estimates of 80,000 to 110,000 killed, wounded or missing. It ended French power east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Württemberg over to the Coalition. Napoleon told the Senate that a year before all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> had marched with him and now all Europe marched against him. The Coalition invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1814, and Napoleon abdicated in May and was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>. A 91-metre Monument to the Battle of the Nations was completed in Leipzig in 1913.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Leipzig” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Austria joined the Sixth Coalition in August 1813, and Field Marshal Karl Philipp zu Schwarzenberg commanded the Allied armies that won the <b>Battle of Leipzig</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The German Confederation (Deutscher Bund) was created at the Congress of Vienna to fill the space left by the Holy Roman Empire, dissolved in 1806, and by Napoleon’s Confederation of the Rhine, which had collapsed after the <b>Battle of Leipzig</b> in 1813.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick abandoned Napoleon after the <b>Battle of Leipzig</b>, and by the Treaty of Fulda of November 1813 the Allies guaranteed his possessions, so that the kingdom survived the Congress of Vienna intact.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After his defeat at the <b>Battle of Leipzig</b>, Allied armies crossed Swiss territory in December 1813 and the Mediation regime collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With some 600,000 soldiers engaged over three days, the '<b>Battle of the Nations</b>' was the largest in Europe before the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-16">16 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Battle of the Nations</b> ended with Napoleon's army in retreat from Leipzig, its rearguard trapped when a bridge was blown too early.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-19">19 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
